<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Panama Has Delicious Food! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Panama Has Delicious Food!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Respond to texts with personal reflections. Reading: Create simple questions based on reading materials. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can respond to texts about meal planning, with personal reflections (e.g., "I like cooking with my family."). Can create simple questions and infer answers from reading materials, using context (e.g., "What ingredients do we need for a healthy meal?" "We need some rice and vegetables."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes by recognizing and applying simple prefixes and suffixes to form new words (e.g., "-un" in "uncountable" and "-ing" in "cooking").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of popular Panamanian foods (e.g., rice, beans, mangos, watermelon, chicken, bread, juice) introduced in Lesson 1. Students call out the names of the foods. The teacher asks, "What do you like to eat for breakfast/lunch/dinner?" to activate prior knowledge and vocabulary related to meals. Students respond with simple sentences like, "I like rice and beans."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a short sentence on the board, like "Fruits and vegetables are healthy." The teacher points to each word as they read it clearly, modeling pronunciation for /f/ in "fruits" and /v/ in "vegetables." The teacher also highlights the "ing" in "cooking" from the vocabulary list, explaining it as a common ending. The teacher demonstrates how to identify key words in a short sentence related to food.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are vegetables good for you? (Yes, they are healthy!)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we eat breakfast in the morning or at night? (In the morning!)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher introduces a short, simple text titled "My Family's Healthy Meals" about planning meals for a family. The text integrates target vocabulary (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, fruits, vegetables, cook, eat, healthy) and grammar (present simple, can, countable/uncountable nouns like "some rice," "many fruits").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt, approximately 50-70 words, printed on a worksheet or projected on the board. The text should be clearly legible and maybe include simple illustrations of food items. Its register is informal, suitable for children's reading.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text:</i><br>
                "My Family's Healthy Meals<br>
                Hello! My name is Ana. This is my family's meal plan. For breakfast, we often eat some fresh fruits like mangos and watermelon. My mom likes to cook eggs too. For lunch, we usually eat rice and beans. Sometimes we can add some tasty chicken. After school, I often eat a healthy snack like a banana. For dinner, we make a big salad with tomatoes and lettuce. We always drink water with our meals. We enjoy eating fresh vegetables. Cooking is fun with my family!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to read short and simple texts related to daily life and personal experiences, identifying key information about meal planning and food preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is Ana's text about?" (Possible answer: Her family's healthy meals.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read the text and decide if statements are True or False.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Ana's family eats mangos for breakfast. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">They eat bread for lunch. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Ana cooks dinner. (False - "Cooking is fun with my family!" implies shared, but mom cooks in the text, so it's a good inference question)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Reading:</b> Students practice reading for specific information, identifying vocabulary, and understanding simple grammar structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of texts about food and meals. Target grammar: Present simple, modal verb "can", countable/uncountable nouns (some, many). Target vocabulary: breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water, juice, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, fruits, vegetables, cook, eat, enjoy, healthy, fresh, often, sometimes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice – Reading and Matching):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from Ana's meal plan (or similar, very short sentences) and a column of corresponding pictures of food items or meals. They read each sentence and draw a line to match it with the correct picture. E.g., "We eat rice and beans for lunch." matched with a picture of rice and beans. This also includes practice with phonemes like /f/ and /v/ in words like "fruits" and "vegetables."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice – Reading and Completing):</b> Students get a short paragraph (similar to Ana's text but with some words removed) with blanks. They read the text and choose the correct word from a word bank to complete each sentence. The word bank includes target vocabulary and quantifiers (e.g., "some," "many"). E.g., "For dinner, we make a big salad with ____ and ____." (Word bank: tomatoes, lettuce, juice, bread). The teacher emphasizes reading the whole sentence to understand the context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction – Peer Reading and Checking):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading aloud short sentences about food preferences or meal ideas (e.g., "I like cooking with my family.", "We can eat some fruit."). Their partner listens and checks if the pronunciation of target sounds (/f/, /v/, -ing suffix in 'cooking') is clear and provides constructive feedback. They also discuss answers to comprehension questions from Activity 1 or 2, verbally checking each other's understanding of the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario:</b> Students will read a new, short text and then create questions based on it, demonstrating their ability to infer information and apply their reading comprehension skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Paired activity for reading and question creation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a new, short text (e.g., "A Panamanian Picnic!") describing a simple picnic with different foods (e.g., "First, we slice the watermelon and mangos. Next, we put some rice and chicken in bowls. We also bring fresh juice and a big bread. We eat many tasty fruits and vegetables.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually, highlighting any words they remember from previous lessons or find interesting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students work together to create two simple questions based on the reading material. Questions should use target grammar (present simple, "What," "Can"). E.g., "What fruits do they bring?", "Can they eat chicken?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair then exchanges their questions with another pair. The new pair reads the questions and tries to find the answers in the original text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, pairs share their questions and answers with the class, with the teacher facilitating and providing feedback on clarity and accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students are actively reading about food and meals in Panama ("Panama Has Delicious Food!") and interacting to "Plan Meals for My Family" indirectly by extracting meal ideas and food items from the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair work and question creation, focusing on individual and collaborative reading skills, as well as peer assessment for question clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually complete a short "Read and Respond" worksheet. They will read 2-3 new simple sentences about food (e.g., "My family cooks sancocho for lunch sometimes. It has many vegetables.") and then:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond with a personal reflection (e.g., "I like sancocho too." or "I want to try sancocho.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Create one simple question about the text for the teacher. (e.g., "What vegetables are in sancocho?")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to read and understand the main idea of simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., "cook," "vegetables") and grammar (present simple, "can," countable/uncountable nouns).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and relevance of the personal reflection.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct formation of a simple question based on the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Application of basic phonemic awareness (decoding words like 'cooking', 'unhealthy' if introduced, or words with /f/ and /v/ sounds).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with these prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about food and meals. Yes / No / A little"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask a question about food. Yes / No / A little"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food words did I read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about reading the texts?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students understand the reading texts and create questions? (e.g., pairing students, clear examples, visual aids).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially for students struggling with decoding or comprehending simple sentences? (e.g., more direct instruction on sight words, simpler texts, more controlled question-forming practice).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on reading texts about food and planning meals. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will use the same vocabulary and concepts to enable students to actively speak and interact in pairs, sharing their own food preferences and meal ideas, using simple sentence structures and asking and answering questions verbally.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>